All five candidates for two San Clemente City Council seats say they support historic preservation and a two-story height limit for buildings along Avenida Del Mar.
But some differing perspectives surfaced during a candidate forum hosted by the San Clemente Historical Society at the Community Center on Sunday.
Candidate Dan Bane, an environmental and land use attorney, said he would not reverse the City Council��s decision to limit building heights to two stories, but he feels flexibility is needed for individual properties where stepped-back architecture going higher than two stories could be done without visual impacts. He said the conditional-use permit process provides effective controls, and he supports the use of shared-parking agreements between landowners rather than waiving parking requirements as incentives for development. He said waivers don��t create more parking, only worsen parking shortages.
Incumbent Councilman Chris Hamm, a firefighter, said he supports parking waivers together with the two-story limit and incentives for adaptive reuse of buildings. He said the city already has shared-parking arrangements but some businesses won��t accept them, so sometimes waivers are necessary.
