UNION CITY — Work has restarted on a long-discussed — and delayed — east-west connector between Interstate 880 and Mission Boulevard, a roadway vital to the city��s efforts to develop the area around its BART station.
��This roadway is critical. It will be an access point for cars coming to and from the BART development,�� said Alameda County Supervisor Richard Valle.
The supervisor, a former Union City councilman, is holding a town-hall meeting Thursday at Union City��s council chambers to update the community on the project.
A key piece of the project is a new road from Paseo Padre Parkway to Mission southeast of the Union City BART station. The new road will have three underpasses so vehicles can pass under BART and railroad tracks.
Sections of Paseo Padre Parkway and Decoto Road will be widened as part of the project, and improvements made where Mission will intersect with the new road. The connector, which lies in Union City on the west and Fremont to the east, will have bike lanes.
The connector has been in the works since 1987, said Union City Mayor Carol Dutra-Vernaci. Design work started in 2009 but was put on hold in 2012 because of a lack of money.
The project will be paid for with a combination of local, county, regional and state funds. It also may qualify for Measure BB money. Alameda County voters approved the one-cent sales tax earmarked for transportation in November 2014, allowing many delayed projects to begin to move forward.
The Alameda County Transportation Commission started work on the connector��s final design in October and expects to complete specifications and refine cost estimates in 2017.
A preliminary estimate puts the price tag at $230.5 million. That includes project development, acquiring rights of way, mitigation and construction, said Tess Lengyel, the commission��s policy and planning deputy director.
The entire connector between I-880 and Mission is more than three miles long, Lengyel said. The commission is estimating construction will end in late 2019.
��It��s long overdue. This connector is significant and crucial not only for our local needs, but the regional needs as well,�� Dutra-Vernaci said. Eastbound Dumbarton Bridge traffic feeds onto Decoto Road east of I-880.
��Our roadways around that area already are extremely congested at peak hours,�� the mayor said.
Union City��s Station District near its BART station has more than 400 high-end apartments, 200 townhouses and 150 apartments for low-income residents.
And more development is planned.
