WASHINGTON -- Michael S. Dukakis promised a campaign about competence, not ideology. George Bush, who beat him, completed the selection of a Cabinet on Thursday that might be described with the same words.
The president-elect's 14 choices for the Cabinet, as well as those for the half-dozen or so other jobs that strongly affect the substance and personality of an administration, resemble the selections of a parliamentary leader more than those of an American chief executive.
Prime ministers choose ministers almost entirely from the relatively small pool of people who have accumulated experience (and presumably wisdom) in prior governments. For all his talk of the need for new faces, Bush has done the same thing.
"It's quite unusual for this country," one of those chosen for a powerful post commented on Thursday. "We usually have government by amateurs here."
With relatively few exceptions, the nominees are white male governmental professionals or semipros. Most of them live inside the Beltway, as the Washington phrase has it, and most of them are pragmatists, which is what people inside the Beltway like.
And most of them, like the man they are going to work for, play tennis. James A. Baker III, Bush's choice for secretary of state, has been his on- court partner since 1970, and Clayton K. Yeutter, the choice for secretary of agriculture, is so proud of his game that he mentions it in Who's Who.
There are not many names on the list that bring to mind the kind of fire- breathing ideologues President Reagan often favored. Former Rep. Jack F. Kemp may be a "movement conservative," but Bush has named him secretary of housing and urban development, a post where Kemp's views on minority economic development, most unorthodox for a right-winger, are likely to come into play.
