His name is similar, and so apparently is his approach to basketball. As Cory Alexander leaves Virginia for the NBA, prized freshman Courtney Alexander enters with that same brand of self-confidence that some will label cockiness.
But Courtney Alexander, like Cory Alexander, doesn't care.
''They recruited me to score, and they recruited me to contribute right away,'' he said during the Cavaliers' media day last week. ''That's what I'm looking to do this season. I'm not looking just to make the ACC's All-Rookie team, I'm looking to make the All-ACC team. Some may disagree with that, but I really don't care. I have high expectations.''
Alexander, a 6-foot-5 freshman from Durham, N.C., who can play either guard spot or small forward, will get his chance. He averaged 31 points per game last season at Jordan High and made 44 percent of his 3-point shots.
''In some people's minds, it's a foregone conclusion that Harold (Deane), Curtis (Staples) and Jamal (Robinson) are going to start,'' Virginia coach Jeff Jones said. ''I think Courtney's going to have an awful lot to say about that. He's that good, but he's also inexperienced and young. It may take some time, but from what I've seen so far I wouldn't put it past him to shake things up a little bit.''
CROSSED OUT. Clemson defensive end Marvin Cross, a fifth-year senior who injured his knee in August and hasn't played this season, will undergo reconstructive surgery next week.
Which probably means his college career is over. Steve Nelson, the Tigers' compliance coordinator, said Clemson will appeal the NCAA for a sixth year but added he is not optimistic.
''The chances are slim,'' Nelson said, ''but we'll talk to them.''
The NCAA Manual gives athletes five years to play four seasons. A waiver is granted for ''circumstances beyond the control of the student-athlete.''
Cross' situation would appear to be very similar to that of Virginia offensive tackle Chris Harrison, who was granted a sixth year after breaking his leg two Augusts ago. Like Cross, Harrison had redshirted his first year.
